   "source": [
    "## Q8. What are the advantages and disadvantages of polynomial regression compared to linear regression? In what situations would you prefer to use polynomial regression?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Answer:**\n",
    "\n",
    "**Advantages of Polynomial Regression:**\n",
    "1. Captures nonlinear relationships\n",
    "2. More flexible and can fit complex patterns\n",
    "3. Better fit for data with curves\n",
    "4. Can approximate any continuous function (universal approximation)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Disadvantages of Polynomial Regression:**\n",
    "1. Prone to overfitting, especially with high degrees\n",
    "2. Sensitive to outliers\n",
    "3. Poor extrapolation capability\n",
    "4. Can be unstable at boundaries\n",
    "5. More complex interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "**When to prefer Polynomial Regression:**\n",
    "1. Data shows clear curvilinear patterns (growth, decay, saturation)\n",
    "2. Domain knowledge suggests nonlinear relationships\n",
    "3. Simple linear model underfits (high bias)\n",
    "4. Limited data points (use with caution)\n",
    "5. Examples: Population growth, chemical reaction rates, physics experiments"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Example: Comparing different polynomial degrees\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error\n",
    "\n",
    "# Generate data with nonlinear pattern\n",
    "np.random.seed(42)\n",
    "X = np.linspace(-3, 3, 100).reshape(-1, 1)\n",
    "y = 0.5 * X.flatten()**3 - X.flatten()**2 + 2 * X.flatten() + np.random.randn(100) * 2\n",
    "\n",
    "# Test different polynomial degrees\n",
    "degrees = [1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 15]\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(15, 10))\n",
    "\n",
    "for i, degree in enumerate(degrees, 1):\n",
    "    # Create and fit polynomial model\n",
    "    poly_features = PolynomialFeatures(degree=degree)\n",
    "    X_poly = poly_features.fit_transform(X)\n",
    "    model = LinearRegression()\n",
    "    model.fit(X_poly, y)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Predict\n",
    "    y_pred = model.predict(X_poly)\n",
    "    mse = mean_squared_error(y, y_pred)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Plot\n",
    "    plt.subplot(2, 3, i)\n",
    "    plt.scatter(X, y, alpha=0.5, s=20)\n",
    "    plt.plot(X, y_pred, color='red', linewidth=2)\n",
    "    plt.title(f'Degree {degree}\\nMSE: {mse:.2f}')\n",
    "    plt.xlabel('X')\n",
    "    plt.ylabel('y')\n",
    "    plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nObservations:\")\n",
    "print(\"- Degree 1 (linear): Underfitting - too simple\")\n",
    "print(\"- Degree 3: Good balance - captures true pattern\")\n",
    "print(\"- Higher degrees: Overfitting - captures noise\")"
   ]
